  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    " ##  Preprocessing"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 1: Read the Data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Machine Learning with Python is often done with three main libraries. The numpy library, which allows data to be stored in matrices (numpy arrays), so that they can be reshaped as needed and fed into regression models as is. 2) The pandas library, which is built on top of the numpy library, contains several functions that allow the data analyst to see the data set in a format similar to R and excel presentations. Additionally, besides being able to keep track of the data on an attribute by name basis, pandas has several functions which can tell us the class distribution of the data etc. Finally the scientific matplotlib library is useful for plotting and has extensions such as the Pearson and Seaborn libraries."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "dataset = pd.read_csv('blooddataset.csv') "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 2: Type Checking"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#See the first 5 rows within the dataframe\n",
    "dataset.head()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "dataset.tail()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "type(dataset)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "dataset.shape"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "type(dataset['whether he/she donated blood in March 2007'])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "type(dataset['Recency (months)'])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "dataset['Frequency (times)']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "dataset['Monetary (c.c. blood)']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "dataset['Time (months)']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Conclusion: We have ensured that our data set has no missing values and that all our features are of the same type. However, we have found one interesting discovery. The units of our data are not the same. The 'Monetary' column runs in the triple digit range while our other columns are single or double digits. This conflicting scale can cause a disproportionate visualization (and therefore meaningless) we saw with our graph and would also produce erroneous results since our machine learning models would assume that all data is within the same scale. Fortunately, a library function in the scikit learn library can automatically scale all our attributes for us! This sub-step will be performed in the coming steps before we apply a machine learning algorithm."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 3: Visualize The Data with Seaborn (use boxplot)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "plt.style.use('seaborn-whitegrid')\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "sns.boxplot(data=dataset)\n",
    "plt.title('Boxplot of Blood Dataset Features')\n",
    "plt.show();"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 4: Exploratory Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**We now have a visual of our data. At this point it is possible to run a learning algorithm. However, such a move would be premature. We could definitely narrow our choice of algorithm down to classification instead of regression, since the response variable (the target feature from the dataframe output a few cells above) has categorical classes, but beyond that we have no information besides pure guesswork.**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Instantiate a kmeans object and choose a value of k. Since we have two class variables of the response/target feature, we will choose a value of two; so two clusters."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 11,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=2, random_state=42)\n",
    "#n_clusters --- Number of clusters that we are looking for"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Now let us identify the dataset's into a matrix of features and an independent vector. Since clustering algorithms are unsupervised learning, we will not use train/test split. Since we can see from the dataset that the last column is likely a target, we will split the others and train k-means on them."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "X = dataset[['Recency (months)', 'Frequency (times)', 'Monetary (c.c. blood)','Time (months)']] \n",
    "y = dataset[['whether he/she donated blood in March 2007']]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "kmeans.fit(X)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We can see in the parameters output that kmeans++ was run by default and 2 clusters were formed."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 14,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "kmeans.cluster_centers_"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "kmeans.labels_"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Now we will also perform hierarchical clustering. With two types of clustering algorithms, we will have a very accurate breakdown of our model's clusters."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 16,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n",
    "sc_X = StandardScaler()\n",
    "X_scaled = sc_X.fit_transform(X)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 17,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from scipy.cluster.hierarchy import linkage, dendrogram\n",
    "mergings = linkage(X_scaled, method='ward')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 18,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))\n",
    "dendrogram(mergings)\n",
    "plt.title('Hierarchical Clustering Dendrogram')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Step 5: Feature Elimination - Reducing the noise of our data"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 19,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\n",
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
    "\n",
    "X_val = dataset[['Recency (months)', 'Frequency (times)', 'Monetary (c.c. blood)', 'Time (months)']].values\n",
    "y_val = dataset['whether he/she donated blood in March 2007'].values\n",
    "\n",
    "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X_val, y_val, test_size = 0.2, random_state = 42)\n",
    "\n",
    "dtree = DecisionTreeClassifier(random_state=42)\n",
    "dtree.fit(X_train, y_train)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 20,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "feature_importances = pd.DataFrame({'Feature': dataset.columns[:-1], 'Importance': dtree.feature_importances_})\n",
    "print(feature_importances)"
   ]
  }
 ]
}
